Subject: Run the AOT profiler on the threaded interpreter

What this does
  The AOT profiler had hooks only in the monolith's dispatch point, so a
  profile build had to be a monolith build and nothing else. This adds its
  hooks to the threaded chain: an op count at the dispatch, a loop trip count
  at the backward branch, a call site at each of the four call handlers, a
  register type at each register store, and an entry count where a frame is
  built. A runtime switch (`QJS_AOT_PROFILE_OUT`, read once at runtime
  creation) gates every hook, so a profiling build with the switch off runs
  the interpreter unchanged.

  The threaded interpreter also rewrites opcode bytes in place once a property
  site is filled. The profiler computes a function's digest at teardown, after
  that rewrite, while the generator computes it on fresh code, so any function
  that ran a quickened op got two different digests and did not bind. The
  digest now hashes each opcode's canonical form (`regvm_rop_canonical`), which
  maps the quickened spelling back to the opcode it was rewritten from.

Why we need it
  The shipping interpreter is the threaded plus record build, and a profile
  must be collected from the interpreter that ships. Today's profiler cannot
  be built on it at all: `#error` refuses, because the op count hooked only
  the monolith.

What it adds
  `AOT_PROF_TICK`, `AOT_PROF_EDGE`, `AOT_PROF_REG_T` and `JSRuntime.aot_prof_on`.
  The threaded default no longer excludes `QJS_AOT_PROFILE`.
  `regvm_rop_canonical`, used by `aot_digest_code`.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.

